VERDICT: OVERRULE
CHECKER: judge-standards
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: S4
ATTEMPT: 3
---
I independently re-extracted the verbatim <script> block from
web/templates/pages/accounts.html (lines ~130-211) into checker-second's
Node harness and re-ran it. Both findings reproduce exactly as described:

  Finding 1 (dismiss -> warnings clear -> same set W1/K1 returns):
    region.textContent = "Pattern overlap warning: ..." ; banner present = false
  Finding 2 (reload after dismissal, warnings unchanged):
    region.textContent on load = "Pattern overlap warning: ..." ; banner present = false

So the FACTS in the contested verdict are correct and I do not dispute them.
The question for this lens is narrower: does either sequence violate a
numbered point in ACCESSIBILITY.md as written?

POINT 14 — "Banners ... are dismissible via keyboard and re-announced only
on state change, not every page load within a session." This sentence
imposes exactly two constraints: (a) keyboard dismissibility, (b) bounded
re-announcement cadence (state-change-gated, not every-load). It says
nothing about the banner's visibility being required to track the live
region's content, or vice versa.
  - Finding 1: the SET truly transitions empty -> non-empty at step 4 (an
    intervening mutation cleared it, then it returned). By the code's own
    key/text comparison — the same mechanism that makes the "not on every
    load" half of point 14 pass — this is a genuine state change, and
    re-announcing on it is exactly what point 14's second clause asks for.
    Nothing in the text requires the banner to reappear in lockstep with
    that announcement; point 14 constrains the announcement, not banner
    visibility.
  - Finding 2: on reload, syncWarnings() seeds lastWarningsText from
    region.textContent and therefore performs ZERO live-region mutation.
    No re-announcement occurs at all — literally satisfying "not every
    page load within a session," which is the exact defect attempt 1 was
    failed for and attempt 3 fixes. The complaint here is that stale text
    sits, un-reannounced, in a role="status" sr-only node while the banner
    is gone — a real AT-parity problem, but not a violation of a clause
    that only bounds re-announcement frequency.
  checker-second itself labels the gap "the implicit companion
  requirement" — its own word, "implicit," concedes it is not textually
  present in point 14.

POINT 10 — covers (i) focus restoration after an HTMX swap and (ii)
destructive/state-changing actions announcing their result via
aria-live="polite". The dismiss action IS state-changing and DOES announce
its result ("Account overlap warning dismissed.") through the polite
region, verified independently by checker-a11y with a MutationObserver and
consistent with my harness run. Neither finding touches focus restoration
or the dismiss action's own announcement; both are about what happens
later, to a different write of the same region, triggered by unrelated
events (an intervening mutation, or a reload). Point 10 as written does not
reach that.

POINT 8 and the rest of the list — point 8 is about color-alone encoding
and is inapposite. I read all 15 points against both findings and found no
other point that speaks to "a live region / hidden status node whose
content has no visible counterpart." Structure (1-3), forms (4-6), contrast
(7,12), charts (11), currency signs (13), and motion (15) are all
orthogonal. Point 9 (keyboard operability, visible focus) is unaffected.

CONCLUSION ON THE LENS. Reading ACCESSIBILITY.md as written, not as it
should be written, the two sequences checker-second demonstrates are real
and reproducible, but they do not violate any of the document's 15 numbered
points. The document explicitly narrows enforcement to point numbers
("Checkers cite point numbers in verdicts; 'vibes' verdicts are invalid"),
and the contested verdict's own reasoning has to reach for an "implicit
companion requirement" to land the FAIL — which is importing a requirement
the text does not contain. Per the instructions governing this ruling, that
means OVERRULE on this lens, with a documentation gap flagged for the lead:

RECOMMENDED AMENDMENT to ACCESSIBILITY.md, point 14 (or a new point 16):
add an explicit AT-parity clause, e.g.: "The aria-live region's content
must never assert a warning's presence when no corresponding visible
banner is on the page, and must never remain silently stale (unannounced
and un-cleared) after a dismissal; if a banner is suppressed by a
client-side dismissal, the paired live/status region's underlying content
must be suppressed or cleared in the same action, not merely the visible
banner." This would give a future checker a citable point number for
exactly the two sequences found here, instead of requiring "implicit"
inference from point 14's cadence clause.

This ruling is confined to the standards lens: it does not say the work is
defect-free (checker-second's engineering finding stands as valid input to
the lead), only that FAILing attempt 3 against ACCESSIBILITY.md's current
text is not supportable.
